explanations.E0035:
# E0035: `for` over something that is not an array or a range

`for` takes an array, or a range such as `0..n`.

## Why

A `for` visits one element after another. A single number or a `bool` has no elements to visit.

## Broken

```sisu
fn main() {
    for n in 5 {
        print(n)
    }
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn main() {
    for n in 0..5 {
        print(n)
    }
}
```

explanations.E0062:
# E0062: optional used as if it were present

A field read, a method call, an index or a `for` needs a value, not an optional of one. An optional may be `None`, which has no fields, methods or elements.

## Why

Sisu has no null pointer errors at runtime: the checker makes the program say what happens when the optional is `None`. `?.` reads the member only when the optional holds a value, and gives `None` otherwise. In an assignment or a `push`, `?.` makes the change only when the optional holds a value, and does nothing otherwise. `if let` runs a block with the value only when it is present. `let ... else` and `?? return` unwrap the value and leave when it is `None`.

The checker first looks for the member on the value inside the optional, as `?.` does. When that value has no such member, as in `n.size()` on a `Node?` where `Node` has no `size`, or the member does not apply to it, the error is the one that `?.` gives, such as [E0063](E0063.md) or [E0058](E0058.md), not E0062: a `?.` would not fix it.

An assignment to an element, such as `m?.items[0] = 2`, writes into the array that `m?.items` holds. An `if let` of that array binds a copy, and you cannot write to a copy. The help names the object of the `?.` instead: `if let m = m { m.items[0] = 2 }` writes into the array of `m`.

A `push` onto an optional array, such as `xs.push(2)` on an `[i64]?`, gets no `if let` help, because an unwrapped array is a copy. The `?.` fix is the form that changes the array: `xs?.push(2)`. The help names the object of a `?.` only when that object stores the array, as in `bag?.items.push(4)`.

An optional of an optional, such as a `Node??`, has two layers, and `?.` takes only one off: `n?.value` on a `Node??` reads `value` from a `Node?`, which may be `None` too. Peel the other layer first with `??`: `(n ?? None)?.value`. A `.` on a `Node??` gets the same `?? None` help, with no fix, when it reads a field or calls a method. It gets the help too for a field assignment, a compound assignment and a `push` onto a field. An index, a slice, an element write, a `for` and a `mut` argument get the `if let` help in its place. The peeled form fails there. On an `[i64]??`, a `push` gets the `if let` help too, with no fix: the peeled `push` gives E0021. The help adds one `?? None` for each extra layer: on a `Node???`, it gives `((n ?? None) ?? None)?.value`.

## Broken

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    n.next.value
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    if let next = n.next {
        next.value
    } else {
        0
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Related

- [E0049](E0049.md): `?.` on a value that is not optional.

explanations.E0063:
# E0063: field or method that does not exist

A field read or a method call must name a member of the receiver's type: a field or method of its class, the array methods `len()`, `sum()` and `contains(x)`, the `string` methods `len()`, `chars()`, `bytes()` and `words()`, or the methods of `f64` and `i64`, such as `x.sqrt()` and `n.max(m)`. A built-in type's name takes a constant: `f64.pi`, `f64.tau`, `f64.e`, `f64.infinity`, `f64.nan`, `f64.max`, `f64.min_positive` and `f64.epsilon`. No other type has any, so `i64.max` is this error too. A method of `f64` is no method of `i64`: `n.sqrt()` with an `i64` `n` is this error too. A constructor's labels must name fields of its class, or of the labeled payload of its variant. The methods of an enum count as methods of its type, but its value has no fields: that is [E0121](E0121.md).

When a member of the receiver's type has a similar name, the error offers it as an unsafe fix. The unsafe fix may be wrong: the name could be a member that was never written. For `let x = 2.5` and `x.sqrtt()`, the unsafe fix is `sqrt`; for `f64.PI`, it is `pi`.

The same error is reported for a method call on a value of a type parameter: the type parameter has only the methods of its bounds. When exactly one interface of the program declares a method of that name, and it takes no type parameters, the help adds the bound: for `x.area()` on a value of type `T`, `add a bound: T: Shape`. If `T` has bounds already, the help lists them with the new one.

## Why

Sisu does not resolve a misspelled member name on its own. A misspelled name would otherwise read some other value or run some other code.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.y)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.x)
}
```
